{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ASSESSMENT OF THE 60s ACQUIRE BUDGET, AND A RANKED REMEDIATION PROPOSAL — FOR OPERATOR APPROVAL. Nothing here is implemented; no change altering locking behaviour has been pushed.\n\n## A. Is a fixed timeout the right shape?\n\n**The budgets are inconsistent and inverted relative to the work they guard.**\n\n| site | budget | measured/structural work held |\n|---|---|---|\n| `event_append.stage_and_commit` / `batch` / `delete_events` | 60s (30×2) | 0.11–0.35s |\n| `txn._acquire_write_lock` (claim, transition) | **30s** (30×1) | 0.9–1.8s |\n| `compact._compact_locked` | 60s | **21.6s – >13min** |\n| `ensures.run_ensures` | 60s | up to 3 `git commit`s |\n| `purge_bridge._commit_deletion` | 60s | one bulk `git add`+`commit` |\n| `fsck_repair._repair_ticket` | 60s | a rename loop (milliseconds) |\n| `push` merge leg | 15s | local `git merge` (+`stash`/`pop`) |\n| `sync.reconverge` | 15s | local `git merge` / `reset --hard` |\n| `inbound_translate` | 30s | two syscalls |\n\nThe cheapest sections carry the largest budgets; the only genuinely long holder carries the same 60s as a 0.13s comment; and `claim` — the operation AGENTS.md tells every agent to run FIRST — carries the *smallest* budget of any foreground write. A holder permitted 60s can therefore starve a claimer permitted 30s deterministically, which is exactly what happened during measurement.\n\n**The waiters are a lottery, not a queue.** `_acquire_fcntl` polls `LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB` with a jittered 50ms sleep (`lock.py:170-186`); `_acquire_mkdir` polls `mkdir` with a jittered 100ms sleep (`lock.py:412-441`). There is no FIFO ordering and no ticketing. With W waiters each release round is won by roughly one of them at random, so expected wait grows with W while the deadline stays fixed. P(timeout) therefore **rises with concurrency**.\n\n**Exceeding the budget is terminal and lossy, not slow.** `LockTimeout` has no retry, no backoff-and-requeue, and no durable spool: `stage_and_commit` unlinks the staging temp and raises. The CLI turns that into `CommandError` (write lost, operator must notice and retry); best-effort emitters — the `COMPLETION_VERDICT` sidecar (`completion_sidecar.py:61-65`), the push-retry merge (`push.py:355`) — swallow it and the write is lost *silently*. Measured: 6 concurrent `comment`s under one holder, 6 lost.\n\nSo: for an append-only event store whose entire value proposition is that every write is a durable shared record, **a fixed deadline whose expiry destroys the write is the wrong failure shape**. Latency under contention is acceptable; data loss under contention is not. That is the real defect in the budget, more than the number 60.\n\n**The alternatives, with tradeoffs:**\n\n1. **Keep the fixed timeout, change what expiry means** — on `LockTimeout`, leave the canonical event in a durable pending spool and let the next successful writer (or `rebar fsck`) adopt and commit it. Events are UUID-keyed and merge by union, so a late commit is safe by construction. *Cost:* a new durable state (\"written but not yet committed\"), and a genuine semantic change — a caller that saw a failure could later find its write present (\"phantom write\"). Needs the operator's judgement, not just a patch.\n2. **Fair queueing (FIFO ticket lock)** — numbered waiters, each takes its turn. Makes wait time `queue position × hold time` instead of a lottery, so a deadline becomes a *meaningful* statement about queue depth. *Cost:* real complexity in the most safety-critical file in the repo, plus stale-ticket reaping, and it must preserve the dual-window fcntl+mkdir contract that `lock.py`'s module docstring calls permanent.\n3. **Adaptive budget** — scale the deadline with observed contention, or wait indefinitely as long as the *holder identity changes* (i.e. the system is making progress) and only fail on a genuinely wedged holder. *Cost:* it hides the real defect behind patience, and unbounded waits are their own operational hazard.\n4. **Blocking `flock(LOCK_EX)` under a SIGALRM deadline** instead of NB-polling — the kernel queues waiters, which is fairer than polling. *Cost:* flock wakeup order is unspecified on macOS anyway, and the mkdir leg still polls, so it buys less than it looks.\n5. **Change nothing about the budget; just shrink the holder.** *Cost:* does not change the failure shape at all — at high enough concurrency writes are still lost. But it is the cheapest change by far and, on the measured numbers, it removes every incident this ticket records.\n\n**My reading:** option 5 first, because a 100× reduction in the only long holder is available for a small, well-fenced change, and because until it lands any budget work is tuning around a bug. Then re-measure with the same 6-writer harness. If writes still fail, the right next step is option 1 (make loss impossible), not option 2/3 (make waiting longer) — the goal is \"no write is ever lost\", not \"waits are fairer\".\n\n## B. Ranked remediation proposal\n\nRanked by measured benefit ÷ risk. Each names where it could break atomicity and the test that would prove it does not.\n\n### R1 — Batch the introducing-commit resolution into ONE history walk (highest benefit, lowest risk)\n`_build_authorship_ledger` calls `resolve_event_commit` once per signed event, each a full `--full-history` walk. A single walk scoped to the ticket dir with `--name-only` yields the introducing commit for *every* event in one pass.\n* **Measured benefit:** 7 walks × 6.78s → 1 walk ≈ 6.8s. On the 75-event case, ~500s → ~7s. Also removes the `S × 10.5s` global-fallback tail.\n* **Atomicity risk:** none — this is a pure refactor of a read-only query; the lock is untouched and the section still covers everything it covers today.\n* **Proof:** a differential test asserting the batched resolver returns byte-identical `commit_sha` to `resolve_event_commit`/`resolve_position_commit` for every event of a fixture store with a non-trivial (merged, compacted) history, including the case the current ticket-scoped query misses and the global fallback catches. The existing `verify-authorship` suite must stay green on a store compacted through the new path.\n\n### R2 — Hoist the ledger OUT of the critical section\nCompute the ledger before `lock.acquire`, then inside the lock verify the fold set is unchanged; on mismatch, recompute the delta for the new events or defer the pass.\n* **Measured benefit:** with R1 this takes the section from 48.1s to ~0.4s (the mutation only). Without R1 it still takes it to ~0.4s but moves 47s of work to an unlocked phase that may have to be redone.\n* **Atomicity risk — the part needing care:** the ledger must describe exactly the set of events actually folded. If an append lands between the pre-lock scan and the in-lock decision, a stale ledger either omits a folded signed event (`verify-authorship` then cannot re-verify it — a real correctness loss) or includes one that stayed live (harmless duplicate). The guard is a cheap in-lock equality check on the sorted `(uuid, size, mtime)` set of fold candidates.\n* **Proof:** (i) a failpoint test in the shape of the existing `REBAR_TEST_COMPACT_RENAME_BARRIER` that injects an append between scan and lock and asserts the pass either aborts cleanly or produces a ledger whose entries are exactly the folded signed events; (ii) an invariant test over random fold sets: `{ledger entries} == {folded events with author_sig}`; (iii) a two-process test that appends continuously while compacting in a loop, asserting no event is ever folded without a matching ledger entry.\n\n### R3 — Make compact-on-close lock-POLITE (smallest change here; independent of R1/R2)\n`_compact_on_close` currently acquires with the full 60s budget. Compaction is pure maintenance — skipping a pass costs nothing durable, because it re-fires on the next close and on `compact-all`. Acquire with a short budget (e.g. `timeout=5, attempts=1`) and skip silently when busy.\n* **Benefit:** a close can no longer queue for a minute behind another close's compaction; the failure mode becomes \"compaction deferred\" instead of \"close blocked\". It also bounds the damage if R1/R2 are deferred.\n* **Atomicity risk:** none to the store. The risk is *policy*: event logs stay uncompacted longer under sustained load, so `compact-all` needs to actually run. Worth pairing with a metric.\n* **Proof:** a test that holds the lock, closes a ticket, and asserts the close succeeds, the ticket is uncompacted, and a later `compact` folds it identically.\n\n### R4 — Hoist identity/attribution resolution out of the `txn` critical section\n`_match_by_email` reduces all 3,515 tickets inside the `txn` lock (0.68s measured, O(store)). The `event_append` path already resolves it before the lock.\n* **Benefit:** small today (~0.7s of a 30s budget) but it is the same unbounded-growth shape as R1, and it removes an inconsistency between two write paths.\n* **Atomicity risk:** the resolved identity must be the one in force when the event is finalized. It is derived from git config plus the identity registry, neither of which this lock protects, so the exposure is a registry edit racing the write — already possible today on the `event_append` path.\n* **Proof:** assert the finalized event's `author_email`/`author_id` are identical resolved inside vs outside the lock across the fixture matrix; plus a test that mutates the identity registry mid-write and asserts the documented deterministic choice.\n\n### R5 — Give `LockTimeout` a holder identity (this ticket's AC2; pure observability)\nOn timeout, read `.ticket-write.lock.d/owner` and include the v2 stamp (pid, host) and the lock dir's age in the exception message. Optionally a `REBAR_LOCK_TRACE` DEBUG log of acquire-wait + hold + call site.\n* **Benefit:** the 2m17s holder on this ticket was identified by inferring from a commit timeline. That should be one line of stderr.\n* **Risk:** none — no behaviour change; the stamp is best-effort and may be absent (report \"unknown holder\").\n* **Proof:** a test that holds the lock in a child process and asserts the waiter's `LockTimeout` message names that pid.\n* **Landable independently of every other item here.**\n\n### R6 — Change what a lost write means (needs an explicit operator decision, not just approval)\nOption A.1 above: spool-and-adopt instead of unlink-and-raise, so a `LockTimeout` costs latency rather than data. This is what would actually close ab54 and the silent-loss half of e1ed's class, and it is the only item that survives arbitrary concurrency.\n* **Risk:** introduces \"phantom write\" semantics (a caller that saw a failure may later find the write landed). That is a user-visible contract change and is the operator's call, not mine.\n* **Proof:** kill a writer mid-timeout and assert the event either lands exactly once or is reported as pending by `rebar fsck`; a fuzz test with N writers and a forced-contention holder asserting `writes_attempted == writes_landed + writes_reported_pending`, with zero silently dropped.\n\n### Not recommended\n* Store isolation per worktree — ruled out by the operator reframe, and the measurements support that: with a single worktree, a 48s–13min hold starves anything.\n* Raising the 60s budget — it would have converted the measured 6/6 losses into 6/6 minute-long stalls, and the >13-minute holder would have blown through any plausible number anyway.\n* Splitting the compaction commit or the claim's STATUS+EDIT commit — both are load-bearing atomicity (`txn.py:324-331` documents that no reader may see `in_progress` without an assignee), and neither is where the time goes.\n\n## C. Suggested sequencing\n\nR5 (observability, land alone) → R1 (pure query refactor, big measured win, no lock semantics touched) → R3 (three lines, bounds the blast radius) → re-run the 6-writer contention harness → R2 (the real fix for the critical section, and the one that needs the failpoint tests) → R4 → then, and only with fresh measurements in hand, decide on R6 and on whether the budget shape itself needs changing.\n\nAwaiting the operator's decision. Ticket left OPEN."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786115941503894001,"uuid":"7568fcec-541e-4218-b15b-55b3ed2469dd"}